
Overview

This project uses an isolated virtual environment to simulate an Active Directory penetration testing scenario.

The environment consists of a Kali Linux attacker machine and a Windows Active Directory environment.

## Lab Components

|System|Role|
|---|---|
|Kali Linux|Attacker / penetration testing|
|Windows Server|Active Directory Domain Controller|
|Windows Client|Domain-joined workstation|
|VirtualBox|Virtualization|

## Network

The virtual machines communicate through an isolated virtual network.

```
                 Isolated Lab Network

                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │ Windows Server│
                    │ Domain        │
                    │ Controller    │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            │
                     ───────┼───────
                            │
                    ┌───────┴───────┐
                    │ Windows Client │
                    └───────────────┘

                            │
                    ┌───────┴───────┐
                    │   Kali Linux   │
                    │    Attacker    │
                    └───────────────┘
```

## Tools

The following tools were used during the assessment:

- Nmap
- PowerShell
- SMB utilities
- LDAP/Kerberos tools
- SharpHound
- BloodHound
- PsExec

## Purpose

The purpose of this environment is to safely practice Active Directory enumeration, attack-path discovery, credential attacks, privilege escalation, and lateral movement without targeting real-world systems.

## Status

The lab is currently being expanded as additional Active Directory techniques are tested and documented.
